Keep PDF objects of different documents apart in pdf_objcmp (MuPDF commit 4ed1a6d, backported to 1.28.5)

This file modifies MuPDF. It is Artifex's fix for MuPDF bug 709849,
commit 4ed1a6dc3b65f25f88818f76f63f940dd7fed620 on MuPDF master ("Bug
709849: Avoid pdf_objcmp matching across documents.", Robin Watts,
committed on 2026-10-20), with its changed lines applied unchanged to
source/pdf/pdf-object.c of the 1.28.5 source release. The Mervin PDF
project added it on 2026-10-20 in place of its own patch of 2026-10-18.
scripts/build-mupdf-linux.sh and scripts/build-mupdf-windows.ps1 apply
it before building. Delete it when Mervin updates to a MuPDF release
that has this fix, either as master commit 4ed1a6d or as a backport
titled "Bug 709849: Avoid pdf_objcmp matching across documents".

PDF resources such as inline colour spaces, shadings and fonts are
stored under direct pdf_obj keys. pdf_make_hash_key does not hash those,
so fz_find_item and fz_remove_item scan the store and compare keys with
pdf_cmp_key, which is pdf_objcmp. pdf_objcmp ignored which document an
object belongs to, and it compared indirect references by number and
generation only. This had three effects when two documents share one
context:

- Wrong output. Two documents that both use the inline colour space
  [/Indexed /DeviceRGB 1 5 0 R], where object 5 holds different
  palettes, rendered with the palette of whichever document loaded first.
- Freed entries. fz_find_item could return another document's entry.
  When that document closes, pdf_empty_store frees the entry whatever its
  reference count.
- Freed xref reads. For equal dictionaries pdf_objcmp looks up both
  documents' xref entries (pdf_get_xref_entry_no_change). The other
  document may be closing on another thread, and pdf_drop_document_imp
  frees the xref before pdf_empty_store removes that document's keys.

The commit makes pdf_objcmp report indirect references and dictionaries
of different documents as different before it compares anything else.
This applies to every caller of pdf_objcmp. Mervin does not call it
itself. Arrays still compare element by element, so an array with no
indirect reference or dictionary in it can still match another
document's equal array. Such a store value comes only from the array's
own contents, and patch 0001 keeps a found value valid when the other
document's close frees the shared entry.
documentsKeepTheirOwnInlineColourSpace in tests/tst_mupdf_store.cpp
checks the indirect references, and
documentsKeepTheirOwnInlineDictionaryKeys the dictionaries.

diff --git a/source/pdf/pdf-object.c b/source/pdf/pdf-object.c
index 452bebf..37c526a 100644
--- a/source/pdf/pdf-object.c
+++ b/source/pdf/pdf-object.c
@@ -620,6 +620,10 @@ do_objcmp(fz_context *ctx, pdf_obj *a, pdf_obj *b, int check_streams)
 		return strcmp(NAME(a)->n, NAME(b)->n);
 
 	case PDF_INDIRECT:
+		if (REF(a)->doc < REF(b)->doc)
+			return -1;
+		else if (REF(a)->doc > REF(b)->doc)
+			return 1;
 		if (REF(a)->num == REF(b)->num)
 			return REF(a)->gen - REF(b)->gen;
 		return REF(a)->num - REF(b)->num;
@@ -633,6 +637,10 @@ do_objcmp(fz_context *ctx, pdf_obj *a, pdf_obj *b, int check_streams)
 		return 0;
 
 	case PDF_DICT:
+		if (REF(a)->doc < REF(b)->doc)
+			return -1;
+		else if (REF(a)->doc > REF(b)->doc)
+			return 1;
 		if (DICT(a)->len != DICT(b)->len)
 			return DICT(a)->len - DICT(b)->len;
 		if ((a->flags & b->flags) & PDF_FLAGS_SORTED)
